cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use Crisis Rehearsal in research, a report or an evaluation, please cite it as below."
title: "Crisis Rehearsal: rehearsal of corporate crises at T-72h with a 30-event public backtest"
type: software
authors:
  - name: "yepinggu66 (PingguYe)"
    website: "https://github.com/yepinggu66"
version: "0.3.0"
date-released: "2026-10-31"
license: "AGPL-3.0-only"
repository-code: "https://github.com/yepinggu66/crisis-rehearsal"
url: "https://github.com/yepinggu66/crisis-rehearsal"
abstract: >-
  Crisis Rehearsal rewinds a real reputation crisis to 72 hours before it became public and
  gives a swarm-simulation engine (MiroFish/OASIS with a local graph adapter) only the
  information that was publicly available at that moment. 7-12 persona agents react for two
  rounds on a simulated social feed, a rehearsal report is generated, and a fixed LLM judge
  scores the report's Top-3 risks against the documented real outcome. The published backtest
  covers 30 publicly documented Chinese corporate crises (2018-2026), each run three times
  (90 runs). Event-mean Top-3 risk hit rate is 82.2% (95% CI 78.5-85.9, t-distribution);
  within-event SD is 0.26/3; only 5/30 events scored identically across all three runs; the
  internal stability metric STA is 58 against a pass line of 60, so the released result fails
  its own stability bar. The number is directional evidence from a backtest, not a forecast.
  The evidence pack, scoring records, failures and limitations are shipped and recomputable
  without an API key.
keywords:
  - crisis rehearsal
  - reputation risk
  - swarm simulation
  - LLM agents
  - backtest
  - reproducibility
  - scenario planning
  - enterprise risk
  - open source
